# What each font type is, which operators will paint with it, and where
# this interpreter and the specification differ.
#
# PLRM Table 5.1 lists the font types. Five are base fonts -- 1, 2, 3, 14
# and 42 -- one is the composite font, 0, and four more (9, 10, 11, 32)
# are not fonts at all: they are values definefont stamps into a CIDFont
# dictionary to say which kind it is, and PLRM 5.9 is explicit that "a
# CIDFont is not a base font".
#
# This family has no dispatch. The type is re-read at thirteen places in
# data/font.ps and two in data/cid.ps, and those places ask only three
# questions between them: is this a composite, is this a Type 3, and does
# this dictionary carry a face. A site asking only half of what it needs
# is not visible in the source -- every one of them looks reasonable
# alone -- so the register asks the question behaviourally instead, by
# crossing every text operator with every font type it can be handed.
# A site that asks half the question shows up as one cell.
#
#   type <code>  <accepted|refused>  <reason>
#
# accepted  definefont takes a dictionary stating this type
#
# PROBED with a dictionary that carries a working face, copied from one
# the host resolved. Each accepted type is validated for what its own kind
# needs: the composite for its mapping and its descendants, the procedural
# one for a way to build a glyph, the TrueType one for the font data a
# TrueType glyph comes out of, and the two charstring formats for the face
# their glyphs come out of. A code that is none of those is refused,
# whether the specification named it and this interpreter does not
# implement it, or the specification never named it at all.

type 0   accepted  the composite font, PLRM 5.9. Validated: definefont asks for the mapping type and the descendant vector, and refuses a dictionary lacking either
type 1   accepted  charstrings in the Type 1 format, PLRM Table 5.1. Not validated beyond the face
type 2   accepted  charstrings in the compact format, which is what a name from the host most often resolves to here. Not validated beyond the face
type 3   accepted  glyphs described by a procedure, PLRM 5.7. Validated: definefont asks for a way to build a glyph
type 14  refused   PLRM Table 5.1 names it Chameleon and nothing here implements one, so definefont refuses it. A program asking for machinery that is not here is told at the operator it named rather than later by whichever one reached the absence
type 42  refused   glyphs in the TrueType format, PLRM 5.8. Validated, and the only type whose validation refuses this probe: a dictionary claiming to be TrueType without the font data a TrueType glyph is read from is not one. A font of this type obtained from the host is a different matter and paints -- see the routes below
type 99  refused   not a font type at all, and here as the control: a code the specification never defined is refused by the same rule that refuses 14, so the rule is a statement about what this interpreter can paint rather than a list of codes somebody remembered

types 7

# Which text operator will paint with which font type.
#
# This is the cross product, and it is the register's centre. PLRM 8.2
# says of kshow that "if the current font is a composite font, an
# invalidfont error occurs", and says the same of glyphshow in the same
# words. Two sentences of the same shape, and the two operators are not
# obliged to agree by anything in the code: each reads the type itself.
#
#   route <operator> <fonttype>  <ok|errorname>
#
# A composite font here is mapped by bytes, so it is handed a string of
# as many bytes as its mapping consumes; handing it one byte exhausts the
# selector and reports a range error that belongs to the string rather
# than to the operator.
#
# Font type 42 is absent from this axis, and deliberately: the type row
# above records that a dictionary merely CLAIMING to be TrueType is
# refused, so the font crossed here is one the host resolved, which is
# what a program actually gets.

