# Where this interpreter and the specification differ, and what is to
# become of each difference.
#
#   <what>  <settled|thorn|heading>  <reason>

# The FontType a CIDFont carries, one per CIDFontType (PLRM Table 5.11).
#
# A CIDFont is not a base font, but every font operator has to treat it as
# one, and this correspondence is what makes that possible: definefont and
# defineresource each stamp the dictionary with the FontType its
# CIDFontType calls for, and from then on the dictionary answers like a
# font. The two numbers are deliberately different -- TrueType glyph
# descriptions are FontType 42 in a base font and 11 in a CIDFont -- so a
# CIDFont that states 42 itself is corrected to 11 rather than believed.
#
# Held to the interpreter's own table in both directions, and each row
# probed through BOTH operators, because the specification requires the
# stamp of both and one of them getting it would look exactly like both.
#
#   cidtype <CIDFontType>  <FontType>  <where the glyph descriptions come from>

cidtype 0   9   charstrings, as in a Type 1 base font
cidtype 1   10  build procedures, as in a Type 3 base font
cidtype 2   11  TrueType glyph descriptions, as in a Type 42 base font
cidtype 4   32  bitmaps installed in the font cache

cidtypes 4

charpath-of-a-procedure-glyph  settled  A Type 3 glyph is described by a procedure, and charpath through one appends only the advance rather than the outline the procedure would paint. MEASURED as a degenerate box. This is not a deviation to correct: the same degenerate box is what that font yields anywhere, to the digit. A glyph built by running a procedure has no outline until it is run, and running it would paint rather than append; PLRM 5.7 acknowledges as much where it excuses glyphs described by images. Changing it would put this interpreter alone among the three


